        <div class="card custom-card overflow-hidden">
            <div class="card-body p-4">
                <div class="mb-4 text-center">
                    <h3 class="mb-1 text-primary">Understanding Volatility in Crypto Markets</h3>
                    <img src="~/assets/images/Understanding Volatility.jpeg" class="img-fluid my-3 rounded" alt="Volatility Graphic" />
                </div>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">What is Volatility?</h6>
                <p>
                    Volatility describes how aggressively price moves over time. Crypto is known for sharp expansions and fast reversals because it trades around the clock, reacts strongly to sentiment, and often has less liquidity than major traditional markets.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">1. Why is Crypto So Volatile?</h6>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li><strong>Lower liquidity:</strong> Smaller books and thinner order flow can make large moves easier.</li>
                    <li><strong>Heavy speculation:</strong> Narratives, news, and sentiment can move price faster than fundamentals.</li>
                    <li><strong>Leverage and liquidations:</strong> Forced liquidations can accelerate already-strong moves.</li>
                    <li><strong>24/7 trading:</strong> There is no market close, so major moves can happen at any time.</li>
                    <li><strong>Regulatory and macro headlines:</strong> Policy comments, ETF news, rate decisions, and legal actions often create sudden volatility.</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">2. Measuring Volatility</h6>
                <p>
                    Common tools to measure volatility include:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li><strong>ATR (Average True Range):</strong> Shows how much an asset typically moves over a period.</li>
                    <li><strong>Bollinger Bands:</strong> Wider bands mean more volatility; tighter bands suggest consolidation.</li>
                    <li><strong>Historical Volatility:</strong> Analyzes past price fluctuations to gauge potential risk.</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">3. Volatility and Risk Management</h6>
                <p>
                    High volatility creates opportunity, but it also increases uncertainty. When markets are moving aggressively, stop placement, position sizing, and emotional control become even more important than usual.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">4. How Volatility Behaves in Crypto</h6>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li><strong>Bitcoin often leads:</strong> when BTC moves sharply, altcoins usually react even more aggressively.</li>
                    <li><strong>Low-cap coins can move hardest:</strong> smaller assets often have the largest percentage swings and the worst slippage.</li>
                    <li><strong>Breakouts expand quickly:</strong> quiet consolidations can be followed by violent moves once liquidity is triggered.</li>
                    <li><strong>Liquidation cascades matter:</strong> long squeezes and short squeezes can create extreme candles in minutes.</li>
                </ul>

                <div class="alert alert-info" role="alert">
                    💡 <strong>Remember:</strong> volatility is not automatically good or bad. It is simply a condition, and your job is to adapt your risk and expectations to that condition.
                </div>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">5. Strategies for Dealing with Volatility</h6>
                <ul class="list-unstyled">
                    <li><strong>Trade smaller positions:</strong> higher volatility should usually mean smaller size.</li>
                    <li><strong>Widen stops only if size is reduced:</strong> otherwise you silently increase risk.</li>
                    <li><strong>Avoid emotional chasing:</strong> fast candles create the illusion of urgency.</li>
                    <li><strong>Use alerts and plans:</strong> decide your levels before the market reaches them.</li>
                    <li><strong>Choose the right environment:</strong> not every market condition fits every strategy.</li>
                </ul>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">6. Volatility Regimes</h6>
                <p>
                    Markets alternate between expansion and contraction. During quiet conditions, breakout traders may wait patiently for range compression to resolve. During expansion, trend traders may do better while mean-reversion traders become more cautious.
                </p>

                <h6 class="fw-medium mb-3 text-primary">Final Thoughts</h6>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    Volatility is part of crypto's identity. The goal is not to eliminate it, but to understand when it is expanding, how it affects your setup, and what adjustments are needed to keep risk under control.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
